/*  The terminal, as a module.

    It is a separate file because the shell needs the same things — a prompt that leaves the cursor
    where it is, and later raw mode, keys and history. Written once here, pulled there.

    Everything in it goes through C, because these are the parts of a terminal that are not in any
    language: they are calls, and axioms are how Cufet makes a call.  */

Define object terminal with () and module:
    /*  Write without a newline. `State` always ends a line, which is right for a program saying
        something and wrong for a prompt — a prompt has to leave the cursor sitting after it.

        ⚠ Flushed on the spot, and that is not fussiness. The interpreter prints through .NET and
        the compiler through C stdio: two buffers over one file descriptor. Whichever of them holds
        output back is the one whose text arrives in the wrong place, and it would arrive in a
        DIFFERENT wrong place on each backend — an oracle divergence rather than a cosmetic bug. */
    Bind void to put, given (the text letters):
        Pull a book on the c-language.
            Define c-language put-support as [#include <stdio.h>
                static int cufet_terminal_put(const char *text) {
                    fputs(text, stdout);
                    fflush(stdout);
                    return 0;
                }].
            Define c-language number written, given (the text bytes), as [cufet_terminal_put(the bytes)].

            /* The count is not interesting — `fputs` wrote it all or the stream is broken, and a
               broken stdout is not something a prompt can do anything about. */
            Cast written on (letters).
        Done.
        Done.

    /*  Whether standard input is a person at a terminal, rather than a file or a pipe. A prompt
        is for somebody who is about to type; printed into a redirect it is just noise in the
        output, and every REPL that gets this right asks the same question.

        ⚠ The first axiom here that has to know what machine it is on. POSIX spells it `isatty`
        in <unistd.h> and Windows spells it `_isatty` in <io.h> — one call, two names, and a
        header that does not exist on the other side. Guarding inside the C is what keeps the
        Cufet above it from having a platform in it at all. */
    Bind fact to interactive:
        Pull a book on the c-language.
            Define c-language tty-support as [#if defined(_WIN32)
                #include <io.h>
                static int cufet_terminal_interactive(void) { return _isatty(0) ? 1 : 0; }
                #else
                #include <unistd.h>
                static int cufet_terminal_interactive(void) { return isatty(0) ? 1 : 0; }
                #endif].
            Define c-language number attached as [cufet_terminal_interactive()].
            Return cast attached is 1.
        Done.
        Done.

    /*  Put the terminal into raw mode: keys arrive one at a time, nothing is echoed, and Ctrl-C
        comes through as a byte instead of killing the program. Gives back whether it worked, and
        a caller is expected to believe the answer — a pipe, a redirect, a Windows console with no
        handle, and a machine with no termios all say no, and all mean the same thing: read lines
        the ordinary way instead.

        ⚠ It does NOT save the old settings to put back. `raw-off` rebuilds a sane cooked
        terminal rather than restoring a remembered one, which is worse for somebody who had
        deliberately turned echo off and better for everyone else — and it is what lets these two
        be separate axioms. Each method gets its own C block, because an object body takes only
        methods, so nothing can be shared between them but the shape of the terminal itself.

        ⚠⚠ An abnormal exit leaves the terminal raw, because nothing runs `raw-off` on the way
        out. `reset` fixes it. Every line editor that disables ISIG has this. */
    Bind fact to raw-on:
        Pull a book on the c-language.
            Define c-language raw-on-support as [#if defined(_WIN32)
                #include <windows.h>
                static int cufet_terminal_raw_on(void) {
                    HANDLE input = GetStdHandle(STD_INPUT_HANDLE);
                    DWORD mode;
                    if (input == INVALID_HANDLE_VALUE || !GetConsoleMode(input, &mode)) return 0;
                    mode &= ~(ENABLE_LINE_INPUT | ENABLE_ECHO_INPUT | ENABLE_PROCESSED_INPUT);
                    return SetConsoleMode(input, mode) ? 1 : 0;
                }
                #else
                #include <termios.h>
                #include <unistd.h>
                static int cufet_terminal_raw_on(void) {
                    struct termios settings;
                    if (tcgetattr(0, &settings) != 0) return 0;
                    settings.c_lflag &= ~(ICANON | ECHO | ISIG);
                    settings.c_cc[VMIN] = 1;
                    settings.c_cc[VTIME] = 0;
                    return tcsetattr(0, TCSANOW, &settings) == 0 ? 1 : 0;
                }
                #endif].
            Define c-language number engaged as [cufet_terminal_raw_on()].
            Return cast engaged is 1.
        Done.
    Done.

    /* Hand the terminal back the way a program that never touched it would leave one. */
    Bind void to raw-off:
        Pull a book on the c-language.
            Define c-language raw-off-support as [#if defined(_WIN32)
                #include <windows.h>
                static int cufet_terminal_raw_off(void) {
                    HANDLE input = GetStdHandle(STD_INPUT_HANDLE);
                    DWORD mode;
                    if (input == INVALID_HANDLE_VALUE || !GetConsoleMode(input, &mode)) return 0;
                    mode |= (ENABLE_LINE_INPUT | ENABLE_ECHO_INPUT | ENABLE_PROCESSED_INPUT);
                    return SetConsoleMode(input, mode) ? 1 : 0;
                }
                #else
                #include <termios.h>
                #include <unistd.h>
                static int cufet_terminal_raw_off(void) {
                    struct termios settings;
                    if (tcgetattr(0, &settings) != 0) return 0;
                    settings.c_lflag |= (ICANON | ECHO | ISIG);
                    return tcsetattr(0, TCSANOW, &settings) == 0 ? 1 : 0;
                }
                #endif].
            Define c-language number restored as [cufet_terminal_raw_off()].
            Cast restored.
        Done.
    Done.

    /*  One keypress, as a number the Cufet above can read without knowing what machine it is on.
        The normalising is the whole point of doing it here: POSIX sends an arrow key as an escape
        sequence and Windows sends it as a two-byte extended code, and a line editor written on the
        raw bytes would have both of those spellings inside it. Up and Down are named rather than
        swallowed because history is the one thing a line editor uses them for; every other key in
        both families still comes back as 0.

        ★ Home and End arrive as the codes Ctrl-A and Ctrl-E already have, because they mean the
        same thing. Two keys for one idea is the terminal’s business, not the editor’s — and it is
        what lets a host that swallows one of them still leave the other working. VS Code takes
        Ctrl-E for its command palette before the terminal ever sees the byte, which is exactly
        the case this covers.

        ⚠ Home and End have four spellings between them on POSIX. Two put the key in the FINAL
        byte (`H` and `F`) and two put it in the first parameter with `~` at the end — which is why
        the first byte of the sequence is kept as well as the last. An arrow needs only the last.

            -1          end of input
             0          a key with nothing to do: a function key, anything unhandled
             1          the start of the line: Ctrl-A, or Home
             3          Ctrl-C
             4          Ctrl-D, and Ctrl-Z, which is the Windows spelling of it
             5          the end of the line: Ctrl-E, or End
             8          Backspace
            10          Enter
            11          Up
            12          Down
            13          Left
            14          Right
            21          Ctrl-U, throw the line away
            32 to 126   that printable character

        ⚠ The numbers above 10 are this book’s own, not ASCII — 11 is a vertical tab and 13 is a
        carriage return to anybody else. Nothing here ever gives back a byte as itself except a
        printable one and the three control keys that already sit where they belong, so there is no
        byte left over to be confused with.

        ⚠ ASCII only. A byte at or above 128 is part of a UTF-8 character arriving in pieces, and
        appending the pieces one at a time would build text that is not any character at all — so
        they are ignored rather than mangled. Typing an accent is a gap, not a silent corruption.

        ⚠ Reads with `read` rather than `getchar` deliberately. The interpreter calls this from
        .NET, which has its own view of standard input; two buffers taking turns on one descriptor
        is how bytes go missing. Unbuffered, there is nothing for either side to hold on to. */
    Bind number to read-key:
        Pull a book on the c-language.
            Define c-language key-support as [#if defined(_WIN32)
                #include <conio.h>
                static int cufet_terminal_read_key(void) {
                    int pressed = _getch();
                    if (pressed == 0 || pressed == 224) {
                        int extended = _getch();
                        if (extended == 72) return 11;
                        if (extended == 80) return 12;
                        if (extended == 75) return 13;
                        if (extended == 77) return 14;
                        if (extended == 71) return 1;
                        if (extended == 79) return 5;
                        return 0;
                    }
                    if (pressed == 13) return 10;
                    if (pressed == 8 || pressed == 127) return 8;
                    if (pressed == 3) return 3;
                    if (pressed == 4 || pressed == 26) return 4;
                    if (pressed == 1 || pressed == 5 || pressed == 21) return pressed;
                    if (pressed >= 32 && pressed < 127) return pressed;
                    return 0;
                }
                #else
                #include <unistd.h>
                static int cufet_terminal_read_key(void) {
                    unsigned char byte;
                    if (read(0, &byte, 1) <= 0) return -1;
                    if (byte == 27) {
                        unsigned char next, first;
                        if (read(0, &next, 1) <= 0) return 0;
                        if (next != 91 && next != 79) return 0;
                        if (read(0, &next, 1) <= 0) return 0;
                        first = next;
                        while (!(next >= 64 && next <= 126)) {
                            if (read(0, &next, 1) <= 0) return 0;
                        }
                        if (next == 65) return 11;
                        if (next == 66) return 12;
                        if (next == 68) return 13;
                        if (next == 67) return 14;
                        if (next == 72) return 1;
                        if (next == 70) return 5;
                        if (next == 126) {
                            if (first == 49 || first == 55) return 1;
                            if (first == 52 || first == 56) return 5;
                        }
                        return 0;
                    }
                    if (byte == 13 || byte == 10) return 10;
                    if (byte == 8 || byte == 127) return 8;
                    if (byte == 3) return 3;
                    if (byte == 4) return 4;
                    if (byte == 1 || byte == 5 || byte == 21) return byte;
                    if (byte >= 32 && byte < 127) return byte;
                    return 0;
                }
                #endif].
            Define c-language number pressed as [cufet_terminal_read_key()].
            Return cast pressed.
        Done.
    Done.

    /*  The one-character text for a character code, which is what turns a key back into something
        that can be added to a line. Crossing back as `voidable text` means it is COPIED out of the
        static buffer at the boundary, so the next call reusing that buffer cannot reach it. */
    Bind text to letter-for, given (the number code):
        Pull a book on the c-language.
            Define c-language letter-support as [static const char *cufet_terminal_letter(long long code) {
                    static char one[2];
                    one[0] = (char)code;
                    one[1] = 0;
                    return one;
                }].
            Define c-language voidable text spelled, given (the number which), as [cufet_terminal_letter(the which)].
            Return cast spelled on (code) but void is "".
        Done.
    Done.

    /*  Walk the cursor back, without disturbing what it walks over. A backspace MOVES; it is not
        a delete, and that is the whole reason a line editor can put the cursor in the middle of a
        line and still redraw the line around it.

        Built as one string and written once. The obvious loop of single writes would be the same
        bytes and a flush between every one of them, which is what makes a redraw visibly crawl. */
    Bind void to back-up, given (the number many):
        Define stick as "".
        Define left as many.
        While left is greater than 0, repeat:
            The stick becomes "{stick}{cast one's letter-for on (8)}".
            Decrement left by 1.
        Done.
        If the length of stick is greater than 0, cast one's put on (stick).
    Done.

    /* A run of spaces, for painting over what a line used to be. */
    Bind text to blanks, given (the number many):
        Define room as "".
        Define left as many.
        While left is greater than 0, repeat:
            The room becomes "{room} ".
            Decrement left by 1.
        Done.
        Return room.
    Done.

    /* How many characters two lines share from the left, which is how much of the screen does
       NOT need repainting. */
    Bind number to shared-start, given (the text earlier, the text later):
        Define reach as the length of earlier.
        If the length of later is less than reach, reach becomes the length of later.

        Define same as 0.
        While same is less than reach, repeat:
            Define step as same + 1.
            Define from-earlier as the characters from step to step of earlier.
            Define from-later as the characters from step to step of later.
            If from-earlier is not from-later, stop.
            The same becomes step.
        Done.
        Return same.
    Done.

    /*  Replace the line on screen, and leave the cursor where the caret says.

        ★ ONE routine draws every edit — typing, deleting, moving, recalling, throwing the line
        away. Each of those only has to work out what the line now IS and where in it the caret
        belongs; none of them knows how to draw. The alternative is a bespoke sequence per key,
        which is how a line editor ends up with the cursor a column adrift after some combination
        nobody tried — there is no combination here, because there is only one drawing.

        ⚠⚠ It repaints only from the first column that DIFFERS, and that is not an optimisation —
        it is the difference between working and unusable. Repainting the whole line meant walking
        the cursor to column one and back on every keystroke, and at a terminal you SEE that: the
        cursor visibly slides to the start of the line and returns before each character appears.
        The text was always right, which is what made it look like a mystery rather than a bug.

        ★ Typing at the end of a line now costs nothing at all: the shared prefix is everything
        before the caret, so there is no movement to make and one character to print. */
    Bind void to redraw, given (the text gone, the number gone-caret, the text drawn, the number drawn-caret):
        /*  Back to the first column that has to change — or to the caret, if the caret is already
            left of it. Printing from the caret is safe either way, because everything between the
            caret and the divergence is identical in both lines. */
        Define same as cast one's shared-start on (gone, drawn).
        Define from-here as same + 1.
        If gone-caret is less than from-here, from-here becomes gone-caret.

        Define to-the-start as gone-caret - from-here.
        Cast terminal's back-up on (to-the-start).

        Define fresh as the characters from from-here to the end of drawn.
        If the length of fresh is greater than 0, cast one's put on (fresh).

        /* Whatever the old line was longer by is still sitting there. Paint over it, then step
           back across the paint. */
        Define over as (the length of gone) - (the length of drawn).
        If over is greater than 0:
            Cast terminal's put on (cast one's blanks on (over)).
            Cast terminal's back-up on (over).
        Done.

        Define to-the-caret as (the length of drawn) + 1 - drawn-caret.
        Cast terminal's back-up on (to-the-caret).
    Done.

    /*  One line, typed. Raw mode — the TERMINAL kind, nothing to do with Cufet’s raw text — means
        every key arrives here one at a time and the echo is ours to do; without it the terminal
        does its own editing and hands over a line already finished. Both give back `voidable text`
        and both give back void at end of input, so the loop below cannot tell which one it got —
        which is the point. The editor is an improvement to how a line is collected, not a second
        way for the REPL to behave. */
    Bind voidable text to typed, given (the fact editing, the series of text past):
        If editing is false, return read a line from the input.

        /*  The line, and where in it the next character would go. `caret` is 1 at the start and
            one past the last character at the end, which is the same counting `the characters
            from` uses — so an edit at either end needs no special case. */
        Define built as "".
        Define caret as 1.

        /*  Where in the history the line on screen came from. `limit` is one past the last
            recalled line, and means the line being typed now — so walking up stops at 1 and
            walking back down lands on an empty line rather than on the oldest one again. */
        Define limit as (the number of past) + 1.
        Define showing as limit.

        Repeat:
            Define tapped as cast one's read-key.

            /* End of input, or Ctrl-D on an empty line. Void is NO line, which is a different
               thing from an empty one, and the difference is what ends the session. */
            If tapped is -1, return void.
            If tapped is 4 and the length of built is 0, return void.

            /* Finishing and abandoning both step out to the END of the line first. The caret may
               be sitting in the middle of it, and a newline printed from there would leave the
               tail of what was typed above the cursor rather than behind it. */
            If tapped is 10:
                Define after-caret as the characters from caret to the end of built.
                Cast terminal's put on (after-caret).
                Cast terminal's put on ("\n").
                Return built.
            Done.

            /* Ctrl-C throws the line away and starts again, the way every shell does. An empty
               line is nothing to do, so the loop below prints a fresh prompt and waits. */
            If tapped is 3:
                Define abandoned as the characters from caret to the end of built.
                Cast terminal's put on (abandoned).
                Cast terminal's put on ("^C\n").
                Return "".
            Done.

            /*  Everything from here only works out what the line IS and where the caret belongs.
                What was showing is kept so the one drawing routine below can replace it. */
            Define gone as built.
            Define gone-caret as caret.
            Define ending as (the length of built) + 1.

            /* Up walks back through what was typed; Down walks forward, and off the end into an
               empty line. A recalled line arrives with the caret at its end, ready to add to. */
            If tapped is 11 and showing is greater than 1:
                The showing becomes showing - 1.
                The built becomes item showing of past.
                The caret becomes (the length of built) + 1.
            Done.

            If tapped is 12 and showing is less than limit:
                The showing becomes showing + 1.
                If showing is limit, the built becomes "".
                If showing is not limit, the built becomes item showing of past.
                The caret becomes (the length of built) + 1.
            Done.

            /* Moving touches the caret and nothing else — which is the point of having a caret. */
            If tapped is 13 and caret is greater than 1, the caret becomes caret - 1.
            If tapped is 14 and caret is less than ending, the caret becomes caret + 1.
            If tapped is 1, the caret becomes 1.
            If tapped is 5, the caret becomes ending.

            /* Ctrl-U: start over without leaving the prompt. */
            If tapped is 21:
                The built becomes "".
                The caret becomes 1.
            Done.

            /* Backspace takes the character BEFORE the caret, wherever the caret is. */
            If tapped is 8 and caret is greater than 1:
                Define up-to as caret - 2.
                Define kept-head as the characters from 1 to up-to of built.
                Define kept-tail as the characters from caret to the end of built.
                The built becomes "{kept-head}{kept-tail}".
                The caret becomes caret - 1.
            Done.

            /* Typing lands on the line you are building, not on the one you recalled — so `showing`
               goes back to meaning "this line" and Down will not walk into the middle of the
               history from somewhere you have since edited. */
            If tapped is greater than 31 and tapped is less than 127:
                The showing becomes limit.
                Define letter as cast one's letter-for on (tapped).
                Define before-caret as caret - 1.
                Define head as the characters from 1 to before-caret of built.
                Define tail as the characters from caret to the end of built.
                The built becomes "{head}{letter}{tail}".
                The caret becomes caret + 1.
            Done.

            Cast one's redraw on (gone, gone-caret, built, caret).
        Until false.

        /* Unreachable: the loop above only ends by returning. The checker cannot know that, and
           a function that might fall off the end is exactly the thing it is right to refuse. */
        Return void.
    Done.
Done.
